      <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight leading-tight mb-4">
        Bitcoin Is Through $80,000 Again. Is the Bear Market Over?
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-slate-600 leading-relaxed font-normal">
        A short squeeze carried bitcoin from $60,000 to $82,000 in two months, and the spot ETFs just logged their best inflow stretch of 2026. The Federal Reserve is pricing a September rate hike, and the four year cycle crowd says the bottom is still coming. Here is what to watch instead of the ticker.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex items-center gap-3 text-sm text-slate-500 border-t border-slate-100 pt-6">
        <span class="font-medium text-slate-800">Sovereign Wealth Engineering</span>
        <span>•</span>
        <time datetime="2026-09-07">September 7, 2026</time>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="space-y-6 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        Last Thursday bitcoin cleared $80,000 again. It had poked its nose above the line at the end of August, fallen back to $78,000, then crossed a second time, and it has been trading on the line since. The session opened near $77,300, dipped under $77,000, ran to $82,281, then settled around $81,200. On the same day the US spot ETFs booked their largest net inflow since January, $731 million, with BlackRock's IBIT taking $454 million of it. A ceiling that had turned back every recovery attempt this year gave way in a single session.
      </p>

      <p>
        I want to say two things about this. The rally is real, and I am glad it is happening. A real rally is still not the same thing as a finished bear market, and the difference matters most to the people who hold their own keys. So before the momentum convinces you of anything, look at what actually moved.
      </p>

      <p>
        The money data is genuinely good. Bitcoin spent the year sliding from a January peak near $97,900 toward a summer low just above $60,000, a fall of roughly 52 percent from the all time high of $126,198 printed on October 7, 2025. Then August flipped the board. The price rallied about 24 percent in a single month, its strongest month since November 2024, on the back of nine consecutive ETF inflow sessions worth about $3.04 billion through August 27, the longest streak since April. The funds have now taken in $3.8 billion across three weeks, the best stretch of 2026, and total net assets sit near $101 billion. Here is the number the victory laps skip. Year to date, the flows are still net negative by roughly $1 billion. The best month of the year only brought the books back toward even.
      </p>

      <p>
        Part of that speed is mechanical, and I will not pretend otherwise. The move from below $63,000 to above $81,000 in August ran on a short squeeze, with billions of dollars in leveraged shorts liquidated along the way. Squeeze-driven buying is real buying, but it runs out as soon as the shorts are gone. The durable test is whether the ETF table stays green after the squeeze has burned out, and that is a September question, not an August one.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a finished bear market looks like</h2>

      <p>
        Now the harder half of the question. What is a bear market, and how do you know when it has ended? Bitcoin's three completed cycles each fell between 77 and 85 percent from their tops. This one has fallen about 52 percent. Fidelity flagged that gap in February and asked openly whether the fabled four year cycle still holds. Galaxy Research went further in June, publishing a data-heavy case that the low had not been printed yet, with a base case bottom of $40,000 to $46,000 before the end of the year. The four year cycle crowd, which loves a calendar date, has circled October 2026 as the end of the bear market. Every one of those calls was made with the price under $65,000, and all of them look awkward at $80,000.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here is what the cycle crowd gets right. Bitcoin's waves have gentled with every pass. The October 2025 top was the quietest on record, with the valuation gauges that screamed at the previous three peaks barely whispering, because the outburst of euphoria had arrived early, in the 2025 ETF mania, and institutions spent the following months grinding the price upward through steady accumulation instead of one blow-off. The same mechanics now show up on the way down. The average price the market paid for its coins sits at about 44 percent of the all time high, against roughly a third or less at past bottoms, which is Galaxy's way of saying that even the same kind of selloff that ended previous bear markets would end much higher this time. The cycle is not dead. It is being arbitraged by allocators, and allocators do not capitulate the way retail does.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here is the uncomfortable part. The largest corporate holder of bitcoin, Strategy with its 847,363 coins, <a href="/insights/strategys-bitcoin-sales-framework-what-it-means-for-self-custody" class="text-amber-600 font-semibold hover:underline">gave itself permission to sell in June</a>, a policy reversal that JPMorgan flagged as a new kind of market risk. When the biggest buyer of the last cycle becomes a possible seller in this one, the floor the market thought it understood quietly moves.
      </p>

      <p>
        The macro backdrop makes that floor shakier. Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh used his first Jackson Hole speech to restate his inflation focus, and by September 2 the market was pricing a September 16 rate hike at roughly two in three. The ten year Treasury yield sits near 4.8 percent, its highest in almost three years. Oil is above $95 after US strikes on Iran and tanker attacks in the Strait of Hormuz. The dollar is at a two week high. Bitcoin has sold off after three of the five Fed decisions this year, which is why the next meeting is the one that matters. The one relief valve so far was Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent's August announcement of expanded long duration bond buybacks, which pushed yields down and the dollar weaker long enough to fill the rally's sails. A policy announcement can move a month. It is not liquidity that lasts.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">Who is doing the buying</h2>

      <p>
        Here is the part that matters if you hold your own keys. The marginal buyer in this rally is the ETF, a paper claim on bitcoin that lives in bank custody, with the in kind redemption plumbing the SEC blessed in 2025. When institutions buy the dip, the bottom gets shallower, and that is good for your balance. It is also a statement about who the market serves now. Your coins did not get safer because the price went back up. Your counterparty risk did not shrink because the flows turned positive. Neither would change if the price falls again next month. The bear market being over, or not, is a fact about the order book. What you hold, and where you hold it, is a fact about you, and the second one should not be set by the first.
      </p>

      <p>
        My honest answer is that the low near $60,000 could be the bottom of this cycle, and a 52 percent drawdown in an era of institutional dip buying is a defensible bottom. But I would not bet my reading of the market on a round number. The bear market ends when the selling structurally stops, and this time that will happen the way the top got quiet, through the passive accumulation of allocators who do not panic. Hold $80,000 through a September hike and keep the ETF table green after the shorts are gone, and the low is probably behind us. Until then, this rally is a short squeeze with better manners.
      </p>

